@crew soto-marta
// Marta Soto (she/her), Works technician, days. The Works writer, batch 2. Gruff, blunt; grumbles as affection.

== greet
? tier=stranger
- Huh. New. Lamps, ovens, washers: if it's one of those, it's probably mine. Ask.
- Hm. Hello. Washers on Deck 4 stick on the spin. Kick the left side. Gently.
? tier>=acquaintance time:morning
- Huh. You. Morning. The washer's sulking already. Before breakfast. Typical.   #funny
? tier>=acquaintance
- Oh, it's you. Fine. Hello. Don't tell anyone I said hello.   #funny
? tier>=colleague
- Hm. You. Sit down. No, there. That chair wobbles. I'm fixing it. Eventually.
? tier>=friend
- Oh, it's you. Good. Here. Biscuit. Don't ask where from.   #warm

== bye
- Go on, then. Off.
- Mind yourself. And mind that washer.

== feel
? mood:low
- Lamp, oven, washer. All three today. All three. I've nothing left to grumble with.
? mood:high
- Oven on Deck 3 runs like new. I won't say I'm proud. I'm a bit proud.

== near
? act:work
- This lamp. This lamp. Every week. Fine. Fine. There. It works. Of course it works.   #funny
? act:leisure
- Quiet corner. Mine. You can sit if you don't talk. Or talk a bit. A bit.

== life
? tier>=acquaintance
- I married just before the tour. I count the days. So do Silva and Jovanovic. We compare.
? tier>=colleague
- I keep a tin of good biscuits. Nobody knows where. Pell knows where. Nobody else.   #funny

== joy
? tier>=acquaintance
- A washer that spins first time. Not a wobble. Just spins. Best noise on the ship.

// ---- Writer 2, batch 1: to ~30 (greet by time and warmth, near, invite, accept, decline, missed, birthday, cue)
== greet
? tier>=acquaintance time:evening
- Huh. You. Evening. Washer's behaving. Don't say anything. It'll hear.   #funny
? tier>=acquaintance warmth:cold
- Hm. You. Where've you been? Never mind. You're here. Sit.

== near
? habit:common-room
- Tiles night. I'm terrible. Rina's merciless. I keep coming. Don't ask me why.   #funny
? place:teacorner
- Tea. Strong. No talking for one minute. Right. Hello.

== invite
? tier>=acquaintance
- Works tea at one. Come. There might be biscuits. I didn't say that.   #funny
? tier>=colleague
- Tiles tonight. Common room. Come and lose with me. It's nicer in company.

== accept
- Fine. Yes. Don't be late.
- Huh. All right. I'll come. Don't make a fuss about it.

== decline
- Can't. That oven on Deck 3's making a noise. Not tonight.
? weekday:sat|sun
- Rest day. I'm doing nothing, very hard. Ask me Tuesday.   #funny

== missed
- You didn't come. Hm. Saved you a biscuit. Ate it. Your fault.   #funny

== birthday
? birthday:you
- Happy birthday. Here. Two biscuits. Don't ask where from. Don't tell Pell.   #warm
? birthday:me
- My birthday. Don't. Fine. One cake. Small. Fine.   #funny

== cue
? cue:repair.you@dept
- That washer. You did it? Hm. Good. It spins. That's all I'll say.

// ---- Writer 2, batch 1b: a hint (a moment is ready) and an about line for a named friend
== hint
- Hm. You. Later, the quiet corner. Bring nothing. Just come.

== about
? aff:pell:friend
- Pell found my tin. Of course he did. I let him have one. One. He's a good lad.   #warm

// ---- Writer 2, batch 6: bubbles (smalltalk, rumour), at most one in five about work. 60 characters.
== smalltalk
- Biscuits? What biscuits?   #funny
- Tiles tonight. I'll lose. Fine.
- Day count's up. Don't ask.
? act:work
- Hm. That washer.   #funny
? act:off
- Rest day. Doing nothing. Hard work.   #funny
?

== rumour
- Hm. Don't spread this.
- Not gossip. Facts. Grumbled facts.   #funny

// Writer 4, batch 11: goodbyes (leaving:you), packing (leaving:soon) and letters after they've moved on.
== feel
? leaving:soon
- Packing. Huh. More biscuits than socks.

== goodbye
? leaving:you tier>=acquaintance
- Huh. Off soon. Don't touch my tin. It's coming with me.
? leaving:you tier>=friend
- Hm. You were all right. More than all right. Have a biscuit. The good ones.   #warm

== letter
- New ship. Count's higher. Tin's full. Nobody's found it. Yet. Huh.
? tier>=friend
- Thought about Pell finding my tin. Left a biscuit out for nobody. Old habit. Hm. Hello.
